theory RHTNVerificationQuery
/*
================================================================================
  RHTN verification query -- symbolic analysis (Tamarin)
================================================================================

WHICH TREE THIS IS.  `wire-only/`: a property a third party checks from the
bytes it receives.  A verifier is handed a query by a party it has no prior
relationship with, and everything it can rely on is in what arrived.

WHY THIS LEG AND NOT PQXDH.  The models README declares PQXDH unmodelled,
and it stays that way deliberately: it is adopted rather than designed here
(design §14.2.4), and analysing it would duplicate its authors' own work at
high cost.  The query leg is the opposite case -- this project's own
construction, landed 2026-09-30, carrying a confusion property stated in one
sentence and checkable from bytes.

WHAT IS MODELLED.  `wire-format.md` §5.6's query, to the point where a
verifier answers:

  1. the subject signs `query_id`, the hash over the query's fields with
     field 6 absent -- subject, querier, ceremony pre-commitment, fuzzed
     profile, template version, and THE ADDRESSED VERIFIER (field 7);
  2. the querier carries `[ query, consent, selection_basis ]` to that
     verifier, on the end-to-end payload path (§7.10.1's kind 7) or, where
     the verifier is the querier's own serving node, on request type 4;
  3. the verifier checks the query is addressed to IT, recomputes
     `query_id`, and checks the consent against it -- §5.6: *"a receiving
     verifier rejects a query not addressed to it before any processing"*;
  4. it answers with a `VerifierResponse` naming that `query_id`.

THE CARRIAGE IS GIVEN TO THE ADVERSARY, and that is the point.  `Out` and
`In`, nothing else: no session, no authentication of the querier.  §5.6 does
say a verifier attributes the querier to the party the transport
authenticated, and that rule is what a rate limit rests on -- but the
CONFINEMENT of a profile and its ceremony metadata to one verifier is
claimed to hold on the bytes alone, and a model that handed the adversary
less could not test the claim.

WHAT IS ESTABLISHED.

  `an_answer_had_the_subjects_consent_for_this_verifier` -- a verifier that
  answers holds a query the subject consented to AND that names that
  verifier.  This is §5.6's *"what makes the consent an authorization
  instead of bearer paper"*, and it is the whole reason field 7 is inside
  the hash.  Mutation-tested: delete the field-7 comparison and it
  falsifies, the adversary re-aiming one consented query at every prior
  counterparty the selector reached -- which is the attack the sentence
  names.

  `an_answer_names_the_query_it_answers` -- the response's `query_id` is the
  one the verifier accepted, so a response cannot be carried across to
  another query.

WHAT IS NOT, and both are recorded as `exists-trace` lemmas that VERIFY,
because the reachability is the finding:

  `a_query_can_be_replayed_to_its_own_verifier` -- the same bytes arrive
  twice and are accepted twice.  Nothing on the wire stops it: the consent
  is over the query and the query does not change.  What stops it is the
  verifier's own answered-set and its per-requester counters (design
  §7.4.1), which are local state a third party cannot check and this tree
  does not model.

  `a_querier_the_bytes_do_not_authenticate_can_ask` -- field 2 names a
  querier and nothing in the bytes proves the sender is it.  §5.6 puts that
  on the transport ("field 2 MUST equal the transport-authenticated peer"),
  which is outside this theory by construction.  The consequence is bounded
  and stated there: a mis-attributed query chooses its own rate-limit
  bucket, and the profile still reaches only the verifier the subject named.

LIMITS.
  - **The profile is opaque.**  What a fuzzed profile discloses is design
    §7.4.1's oracle-leakage argument, answered by rate limits and by the
    subject holding the capture keys -- a quantitative argument no symbolic
    model expresses.
  - **The grant is not modelled.**  A verifier with no capture key answers
    `unavailable` (§5.5); the key's release is the subject's separate act on
    the same path, and its refusal is what the rate limit IS (design
    §7.4.1).  Nothing about the query's confinement depends on it.
  - **One ceremony, one profile** is a verifier-side rule over its own
    state ("MUST reject one whose fuzzed profile differs from another
    countersigned under the same ceremony pre-commitment"), so it belongs
    to `compliant/`, not here.
================================================================================
*/

begin

builtins: hashing, signing

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IDENTITIES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A public-key registry, as the wire theories build one: the key is public,
  and only its holder signs under it.
*/

rule Register_Identity:
    [ Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ Registered($X) ]->
    [ !Ltk($X, ~sk), !Pk($X, pk(~sk)), Out(pk(~sk)) ]

rule Reveal_Ltk:
    [ !Ltk($X, ~sk) ]
  --[ Compromised($X) ]->
    [ Out(~sk) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE SUBJECT CONSENTS (§5.6)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  `query_id` is the hash over the query's fields with field 6 absent, and
  FIELD 7 -- the addressed verifier -- is one of them.  The subject signs
  that id and nothing else: §5.6 says the signature is over `query_id`, not
  over the query, so the record can carry the signature without the 4 KB
  profile.

  The subject consents once per verifier, which is what the id being
  per-addressee buys: "One consent per verifier follows".
*/

rule Subject_Consents:
    let qid = h(<'query', $S, $Q, ~pc, ~profile, 'v1', $V>)
    in
    [ Fr(~pc), Fr(~profile), !Ltk($S, ~skS) ]
  --[ Consented($S, qid, $V), ConsentedFor($S, $Q, $V, qid) ]->
    [ Out(<'query', $S, $Q, ~pc, ~profile, 'v1', $V>)
    , Out(sign(qid, ~skS))
    , Consent($S, $Q, $V, <'query', $S, $Q, ~pc, ~profile, 'v1', $V>,
              sign(qid, ~skS), qid) ]

/*
  THE QUERIER CARRIES IT (§5.6, §7.10.1 kind 7).  The body is the triple:
  the query, the consent beside it and never inside it, and the selector's
  own `selection_basis` claim, which travels outside every signature
  because the transport authenticates the party making it.
*/

rule Querier_Sends:
    [ Consent($S, $Q, $V, query, consent, qid) ]
  --[ Sent($Q, $V, qid) ]->
    [ Out(<query, consent, '0'>) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE VERIFIER ANSWERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Three checks, in §5.6's own order: the query is addressed to this verifier
  BEFORE any processing; the `query_id` is recomputed from the query rather
  than read out of it; and the consent verifies against the recomputation
  under the subject the query names.

  The recomputation is why field 6 cannot be trusted as it arrives -- a map
  cannot contain its own hash, and a verifier that read the id instead of
  deriving it would be taking the querier's word for what the subject
  signed.
*/

rule Verifier_Answers:
    let qid = h(<'query', S, Q, pc, profile, tplv, addressed>)
    in
    [ In(<<'query', S, Q, pc, profile, tplv, addressed>, consent, sb>)
    , !Pk(S, pkS)
    , !Ltk($V, ~skV) ]
  --[ Answered($V, qid, S, Q)
    , Eq(addressed, $V)                  // (a) addressed to this verifier
    , Eq(verify(consent, qid, pkS), true) // (b) the subject's consent, over
    ]->                                   //     the id recomputed here
    [ Out(<'response', $V, S, qid, 'match', sign(<'response', $V, S, qid>, ~skV)>) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LEMMAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

// The construction runs at all.
lemma a_query_is_answered:
  exists-trace
  "Ex V qid S Q #i. Answered(V, qid, S, Q) @ #i"

// THE PROPERTY.  An answer rests on a consent the subject gave for THIS
// verifier -- §5.6's authorization rather than bearer paper.  Mutation:
// delete check (a) and this falsifies.
lemma an_answer_had_the_subjects_consent_for_this_verifier:
  all-traces
  "All V qid S Q #i.
     Answered(V, qid, S, Q) @ #i
     ==> (Ex #j. Consented(S, qid, V) @ #j & #j < #i)
       | (Ex #k. Compromised(S) @ #k)"

// A response names the query it answers, so one cannot be carried across.
lemma an_answer_names_the_query_it_answers:
  all-traces
  "All V qid S Q #i.
     Answered(V, qid, S, Q) @ #i
     ==> Ex Sx Qx pc profile tplv.
           qid = h(<'query', Sx, Qx, pc, profile, tplv, V>)"

// NOT PREVENTED, and the reachability is the finding: the same query
// arrives twice at its own verifier and is answered twice.  The refusal is
// the verifier's own answered-set (design §7.4.1), local state no third
// party reads.
lemma a_query_can_be_replayed_to_its_own_verifier:
  exists-trace
  "Ex V qid S Q #i #j. Answered(V, qid, S, Q) @ #i
                     & Answered(V, qid, S, Q) @ #j
                     & not(#i = #j)"

end
